# The Gauss-Bonnet theorem, which ties the local bending of a surface to its global shape. For a geodesic triangle the
# area equals the angle defect, the amount by which the angle sum falls short of a straight-line triangle. On a flat plane
# the defect is zero and angles sum to a half turn; on a negatively curved hyperbolic plane every triangle sums to LESS
# than a half turn, so it has a positive area equal to that shortfall, and an ideal triangle whose three vertices reach
# the boundary at infinity has all three angles zero and the maximal area of one half turn. On a positively curved surface
# the angles sum to MORE. The global form of the theorem sums these defects over a whole closed surface and finds the same
# answer every time: the total is two turns times the Euler characteristic, a topological invariant blind to how the
# surface is bent. The discrete version lives on polyhedra, where the defect concentrates at the vertices. Measuring
# angles in thirds of a half turn, a full turn is six units, and the three triangular Platonic solids all give the same
# total defect twelve, equal to six times the Euler characteristic two of the sphere: the tetrahedron with four vertices
# short by three each, the octahedron with six vertices short by two each, the icosahedron with twelve vertices short by
# one each. This file checks all three totals against six times two, and the ideal-triangle area as a full defect.

# The tetrahedron: each of 4 vertices meets 3 triangles, angle sum 3 units, short of a full turn 6 by defect 6 - 3 = 3
# units, total defect 4 * 3 = 12.
hold tetrahedron-total-defect-is-twelve
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 4
      code 3
    call multiply
      code 6
      code 2

# The octahedron: each of 6 vertices meets 4 triangles, defect 6 - 4 = 2 units, total 6 * 2 = 12, the same sphere total.
hold octahedron-total-defect-is-twelve
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 6
      call subtract
        code 6
        code 4
    call multiply
      code 6
      code 2

# The icosahedron: each of 12 vertices meets 5 triangles, defect 6 - 5 = 1 unit, total 12 * 1 = 12, again six times the
# Euler characteristic two, independent of how many vertices carry the curvature.
hold icosahedron-total-defect-is-twelve
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 12
      call subtract
        code 6
        code 5
    call multiply
      code 6
      code 2

# The hyperbolic ideal triangle has all three vertices at infinity with angle zero, so its area is the full defect of a
# half turn, three units, the maximum a triangle can hold. In the same thirds-of-a-half-turn units a half turn is three,
# and the angle sum zero leaves the whole three as area.
hold ideal-triangle-area-is-a-full-half-turn
  call is-equal
    code 3
    call subtract
      code 3
      code 0
